e risk in the balance sheet they  still have to build up equity capital  they still have to find a business model  in some cases to have a sustainable  model in the long term and here is a  major challenge and this problem the ECB  will not be able to solve we really need  to tackle the banking problem in Europe  so does the ECB really has the right  tools from your point of view to solve  these kind of problems you were just  mentioning the EC we can only do its  part in solving the problem and this is  to provide as much liquidity as possible  against good collateral to banks in  order for them to pass it on but clearly  now it's really the political side that  has to act has to close down banks that  are not solvent has to recapitalize  Bangz has to really have a much stronger  consolidation of the banking sector in  Europe in particular in the crisis  countries in southern Europe so the ball  is really the court of the policy makers  of politics in Europe it's not in the  quarter of the ECB the ECB really has  done pretty much all or almost all it  can do is now the responsibility of a  political side we need to take action so  what kind of structural problems does  the eurozone still have we have a  structural problem in a lot of the  crisis countries that they need to have  a change in the structure of the economy  look at Spain which are huge real estate  sector before the crisis we need to  basically find jobs of these people who  lost their jobs in this area in other  sectors  that means crisis countries need to  develop a new economic structure need to  find sectors where they can export there  can be competitive in global markets we  need to solve a number of issues on the  European level banking is one issue we  need to have a more efficient the better  functioning banking sector in Europe  overall we need to have more  coordination on fiscal policy  government's need to stick to the rules  that is very important to regain  confidence in financial markets among  companies among households also here we  need more cooperation in a closer  coordination of policy but have you seen  any progress in these matters sue we  have seen a little progress we have seen  a bit on fiscal policy we have a two  pack a six pack if it's compact in  Europe but in my view it is too little  because if a country still doesn't want  to stick to the rules there's no way for  other Europeans to really make it  binding so we have a lack of binding  rules I believe we we need to have a  fiscal insolvency mechanism so that also  governments in the future can become  bankrupt so this is important because  financial markets then would really  discipline governments much more  strongly at the moment they don't do  that so their number of reforms that are  missing and we need to implement mm-hmm  for many people lower interest rates  means a direct financial loss so what  should people do with their money as a  general rule you should always diversify  your savings you should never put all  eggs in one basket you should always  defer to find that means you should of  course have a saving account but you  should also think of investing in small  shiny equities you should think of maybe  investing abroad because you diversify  also globally and also real estate at  least in some countries like Germany at  the moment is attractive so  diversification is the answer and  clearly you need to to think long term  in the short term equities are risky but  in the long term we know from all  studies that in  thing in equities actually is a sensible  strategy as part of an overall same  strategy now despite the low interest  rates the euro is fairly strong is that  a problem  I think it's a minor problem a lot of  European politicians make it into a big  story and say it's because of the strong  euro that our companies cannot export  and we cannot grow as an economy I think  it's an excuse I think the euros made a  scapegoat for the own failings in  policymaking in those countries clearly  the euro is probably a bit too strong at  the moment it could be five percent ten  percent weaker but the problem that we  have in many European economies is not  that the euros too strong but is that we  have a lack of structural reforms that  we don't have enough flexibility in  those economies that we don't have the  right policies right regulation right  incentives from the government side that  we have still fiscal policy that is not  sustainable so that's where the main  problems lie it's not the euro it's easy  for politicians to blame that because  they can say I have nothing to do with  it but as a general piece of advice that  we as economist should give to  policymakers is focus on those reforms  you can do focus on structural reforms  get your economy to gain competitiveness  and the euro is really the least of our  problems at the moment so lots needs to  be done thank you very much for these  insights thank you
 

